<br>
<br>
<span class=3D"head2" id=3D"WINARGV">3.&nbsp;&nbsp;How does a C/C++ program=
 on Windows get <font color=3D"#00008B">argv[]</font>?</span>
<br>The C/C++ compiler which compiles the program secretly adds extra code =
to
the executable that retrieves and parses the command line to extract
the parameters before calling WinMain (or main). Thus, for a C/C++
executable on Windows, the parameter parsing rules are <b>determined by
the C/C++ compiler that compiled the program.</b>

<br>
<br>
<br>
<span class=3D"head2" id=3D"WINNOSTANDARD">4.&nbsp;&nbsp;Everyone Parses Di=
fferently</span>
<br>
You'll get different results if you pass a command line to
ShowParams.exe (written in C/C++), ShowParams.vbs (VBScript),
or ShowParams.bat (batch file):

<br><br>
<b id=3D"WINSAMP">Sample Code:</b>

<ul class=3D"cmenu">
<li class=3D"expand">ShowParams.c
<ul style=3D"display:none">
<li><span class=3D"code">#include "stdafx.h"
<br>int main(int argc, char* argv[])
<br>{
<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;for (int i =3D 0; i &lt; argc; ++i)
<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;{
