<div class=3D"head2" id=3D"WSH">10.
<center>How To Pass A Parameter to:
<br><span style=3D"font-size:larger">a VBScript, JScript, or WSH Script<br>=
from the Command Line</span></center></div>
VBScript (.vbs), JScript<a name=3D"S9" href=3D"https://www.daviddeley.com/a=
utohotkey/parameters/parameters.htm#R9" class=3D"footnote"><sup>9</sup></a>=
 (.js),
and WSH (.wsh) scripts are run by program wscript.exe,
which is the Microsoft<span style=3D"font-size:xx-small;vertical-align:50%;=
">=C2=AE</span> Windows<span style=3D"font-size:xx-small;vertical-align:50%=
;">=C2=AE</span> Script Host (WSH).

WSH is a language-independent scripting host for Windows Script compatible =
scripting engines.
Microsoft provides both Microsoft Visual Basic Script and JScript scripting=
 engines with WSH.
Windows Script Host executes scripts that exist outside an HTML or ASP page=
 and that stand on their own as text files.<a name=3D"S10" href=3D"https://=
www.daviddeley.com/autohotkey/parameters/parameters.htm#R10" class=3D"footn=
ote"><sup>10</sup></a>

<pre>     Example:
      &gt; C:\WINDOWS\system32\wscript.exe ShowParams.vbs hello goodbye Fri=
day
</pre>

When you run ShowParams.vbs you'll notice the window title says, "Window Sc=
ript Host".
<br>
<br>

<center>
<!-- <TABLE BORDER=3D1 WIDTH=3D"75%" CELLPADDING=3D"15"> -->
<table width=3D"75%" cellpadding=3D"10" style=3D"border:1px solid #ccc;">
  <tbody><tr><td class=3D"verdana">
=E2=80=9CIf you want to get picky, the truth is that you <i>can=E2=80=99t</=
i> read
command-line arguments using VBScript; that=E2=80=99s because VBScript
doesn=E2=80=99t know anything about command-line arguments. But
that=E2=80=99s all right; after all, VBScript doesn=E2=80=99t <i>have</i>
to know anything about command-line arguments. That=E2=80=99s because
Windows Script Host takes care of all that stuff.

<p>
=E2=80=9CAny time you supply a command-line argument to a script that runs =
under
Windows Script Host (that includes JScript scripts as well as VBScript
scripts) those arguments are automatically stored in the Wscript.Arguments
collection.=E2=80=9D

<br>
<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;=E2=80=94<small>Hey, Scripting Guy! 2008 Wint=
er Scripting Games: Retrieving Command-Line Arguments
<br><!-- &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<A HRE=
