<a name=3D"WSHRULES"><span class=3D"head3">10.3&nbsp;&nbsp;Putting it toget=
her:</span></a>
<br>
When you launch a VBScript, JScript, or WSH script from the command line,
your command line goes through two parsers:
<ol>
  <li>First the cmd.exe command line parser parses your command &amp; param=
eters, using its cmd.exe parsing rules. It builds a command line, and calls=
 CreateProcess() passing it the command line it built.
  </li><li>Then the Windows Script Host retrieves that resulting command li=
ne and parses off the parameters, using its WSH parsing rules.
</li></ol>

So to get a parameter into a VBScript, JScript, or WSH script, you need to
work backwards through the two parsers it will go through:
<ol>
  <li>Start with the parameter you want
  </li><li>Apply the WSH parsing rules that Windows Script Host will apply =
when parsing the command line it retrieves
  </li><li>Then apply the command line parsing rules that cmd.exe will appl=
y when you enter the command and parameters
</li></ol>

<b>Step Details:</b>
<br>The steps to create your parameter are:
<div class=3D"ms12">
<ol>
<li>Apply the WSH parsing rules:
  <ol style=3D"list-style-type:lower-alpha;">
    <li>if there are any spaces or tabs within a parameter, add some " so t=
hey are enclosed in a double quoted part
      <ul style=3D"padding-left:10px;list-style-type:none;font-size:11px;">
        <li><span class=3D"ucolor">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbs=
p;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&=
nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbs=
p;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&=
nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;=E2=
=94=8C=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=
=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=90=
</span>
          <br>=E2=80=A2you may enclose the entire parameter in double quote=
s: <span class=3D"codecolor">"Call Me Ishmael"</span>
        </li><li><span class=3D"ucolor">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp=
;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&n=
bsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp=
;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&n=
bsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp=
;&nbsp;&nbsp;=E2=94=8C=E2=94=80=E2=94=90&nbsp;&nbsp;=E2=94=8C=E2=94=80=E2=
=94=90</span>
          <br>=E2=80=A2or you may enclose just the spaces in double quotes:=
 <span class=3D"codecolor">Call" "Me" "Ishmael</span>
        </li><li><span class=3D"ucolor">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp=
;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&n=
bsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp=
;&nbsp;&nbsp;=E2=94=8C=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=
=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=90</span>
          <br>=E2=80=A2or you can make a mess of it: <span class=3D"codecol=
or">Ca"ll Me Is"hmael</span>
      </li></ul>
  </li></ol>
  <br>
</li><li>Apply the Command Line Parser (cmd.exe) parsing rules
  <ol style=3D"list-style-type:lower-alpha;">
    <li>determine what cmd.exe will see as the double quoted parts</li>
    <li>escape the special characters &lt; &gt; | &amp; ^ not in a double q=
uoted part
      <ul style=3D"padding-left:10px;list-style-type:none;font-size:11px;">
        <li>=E2=80=A2the escape character for cmd.exe is ^</li>
        <li>=E2=80=A2it may also be necessary to escape ( ) @ !</li>
        <li>=E2=80=A2it's OK to escape everything</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>If your command line will be placed in a batch file, double the % c=
haracter<a name=3D"S11" href=3D"https://www.daviddeley.com/autohotkey/param=
eters/parameters.htm#R11"><sup>11</sup></a></li>
  </ol>
</li></ol>
</div>

