<div class=3D"head2" id=3D"PERL">11.<center>How To Pass A Parameter to
<br>a Perl script from the command line</center></div>

ActivePerl<a name=3D"S12" href=3D"https://www.daviddeley.com/autohotkey/par=
ameters/parameters.htm#R12" class=3D"footnote"><sup>12</sup></a> and Strawb=
erry Perl<a name=3D"S13" href=3D"https://www.daviddeley.com/autohotkey/para=
meters/parameters.htm#R13" class=3D"footnote"><sup>13</sup></a> both call <=
span class=3D"code95b">MSVCRT.dll!__getmainargs</span>
which follows the Microsoft C/C++ Parameter Parsing Rules.
Therefore the steps are:
<ol>
  <li>Start with the parameter you want
  </li><li>Apply the Microsoft C/C++ parsing rules that ShowParams.exe will=
 apply when parsing the command line it retrieves&nbsp;&nbsp;[<a style=3D"f=
ont-size:smaller;text-decoration:none;" href=3D"https://www.daviddeley.com/=
autohotkey/parameters/parameters.htm#CPP"><span class=3D"nobr">sec. 5</span=
></a>]
  </li><li>Apply the command line parsing rules that cmd.exe will apply whe=
n you enter the command and parameters&nbsp;&nbsp;[<a style=3D"font-size:sm=
aller;text-decoration:none;" href=3D"https://www.daviddeley.com/autohotkey/=
parameters/parameters.htm#WINCMDRULES"><span class=3D"nobr">sec. 7</span></=
a>]
</li></ol>
See sec. 8: <a href=3D"https://www.daviddeley.com/autohotkey/parameters/par=
ameters.htm#CPP">How To Pass A Parameter to: a C/C++ Program from the Comma=
nd Line</a> above.

<br><br>
Sample 'Show Parameters' Perl script:
<ul class=3D"cmenu">
<li class=3D"expand">ShowParams.pl
<ul style=3D"display:none">
<li class=3D"code">if ( $#ARGV &lt; 0 ) {
<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;print "No parameters\n";
<br>}
<br>else {
<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;my $i =3D 0;
<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;my $Parms =3D "";
<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;while ( $i &lt;=3D $#ARGV ) {
<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;$Parms .=3D "Param $i =3D $ARGV[$i]\n";
<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;$i ++;
<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;}
<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;print $Parms;
<br>}
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>


